[SPEAKER_03]: If you're thinking about the nature of it, then there will be like, māna would be the actual thoughts in regard to the sense of the mind, but the nature will be a reflection upon that particular sense of the mind.

726
00:51:20,086 --> 00:51:24,829
[SPEAKER_03]: But you don't really have to worry about much of a distinction between mind and māna and citta and stuff.

727
00:51:25,410 --> 00:51:26,650
[SPEAKER_03]: All you have to do is just be sone.

728
00:51:32,674 --> 00:51:36,497
[SPEAKER_02]: So, we think that the most essential essence is to learn this

729
00:51:37,140 --> 00:51:50,945
[SPEAKER_03]: Seeing things peripherally, to step back and not to be involved with particular... Yeah, not to disregard the particular either, but to learn how to see beyond it, while particular is still present.

730
00:51:52,646 --> 00:51:52,926
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

731
00:51:53,246 --> 00:51:57,847
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's how your mind expands, literally.

732
00:51:58,888 --> 00:52:03,629
[SPEAKER_03]: Because mind is bound whatever you're looking at, whatever you smell, whatever occupies your attention.

733
00:52:04,070 --> 00:52:04,970
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what people see.

734
00:52:06,798 --> 00:52:13,023
[SPEAKER_03]: And learning, while that's present, to see what's beyond it, simultaneously present while that is there, makes your mind bigger.

735
00:52:13,463 --> 00:52:13,864
[SPEAKER_03]: Literally.

736
00:52:14,544 --> 00:52:16,105
[SPEAKER_02]: And how much you can do it, you know?

737
00:52:16,165 --> 00:52:17,006
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you keep it continuously?

738
00:52:17,026 --> 00:52:17,687
[SPEAKER_03]: Indefinitely.

739
00:52:18,147 --> 00:52:19,568
[SPEAKER_03]: The growth can be indefinitely.

740
00:52:20,389 --> 00:52:25,593
[SPEAKER_03]: But you can do it to the extent necessary for the final knowledge, as Satipaṭṭhāna Buddha said.

741
00:52:25,893 --> 00:52:31,457
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it seems that probably at the beginning, it's only like, when you remember, you just go here and there.

742
00:52:34,815 --> 00:52:39,698
[SPEAKER_03]: It's just being dragged each time, whenever something new arises.

743
00:52:40,859 --> 00:52:56,550
[SPEAKER_03]: And, discerning that peripheral background correctly as a phenomena, you can also... you can also arrive at it with the... by the recognition of the phenomenon of enduring of an arisen thing, or of the thing that's ceasing.

744
00:52:56,870 --> 00:52:57,351
[SPEAKER_03]: Enduring.

745
00:52:58,211 --> 00:53:02,154
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that means you kind of have to stay with it, but not fully particularly into it.

746
00:53:02,662 --> 00:53:05,703
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you have to see it changing while remaining the same.

747
00:53:06,323 --> 00:53:10,904
[SPEAKER_03]: You have to see it on two levels, so to speak, simultaneously present.

748
00:53:10,924 --> 00:53:12,324
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why you can approach it through that angle.

749
00:53:13,524 --> 00:53:16,885
[SPEAKER_03]: Like the three, the three sort of aspects in the manifest.

750
00:53:18,625 --> 00:53:24,066
[SPEAKER_03]: Or just arising, or just... Or something said, oh, there is a monk who is very proficient in arising.

751
00:53:25,427 --> 00:53:26,647
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's enough for our country.

752
00:53:26,667 --> 00:53:28,507
[SPEAKER_03]: There is a monk who is very proficient in ceasing.

753
00:53:29,207 --> 00:53:29,368
[SPEAKER_03]: Why?

754
00:53:29,388 --> 00:53:31,588
[SPEAKER_03]: Because he uses the persisting while changing,

755
00:53:32,421 --> 00:53:34,123
[SPEAKER_03]: Because ceasing is manifesting.

756
00:53:34,143 --> 00:53:35,103
[SPEAKER_03]: Arising is manifesting.

757
00:53:59,368 --> 00:54:03,649
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not like ceasing is the actual cessation that you witness, a destruction of things.

758
00:54:06,229 --> 00:54:12,491
[SPEAKER_03]: Because otherwise, if it's a destruction of a thing, then that's what's manifesting, that's what has arisen.

759
00:54:13,571 --> 00:54:16,672
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you can see that destruction can take place only on a particular level.

760
00:54:17,772 --> 00:54:27,414
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like a thing being destroyed, like changing a shape, still remaining a thing, or change becoming a different thing, because it's not the same thing from before, but it's still a thing that came

761
00:54:29,376 --> 00:54:32,659
[SPEAKER_03]: In other words, it's not... cessation does not mean the ending.

762
00:54:32,679 --> 00:54:37,122
[SPEAKER_03]: It just means a different direction from arising, so to speak.

763
00:54:37,763 --> 00:54:40,525
[SPEAKER_03]: Increasing or ceasing, either way, existing or changing.

764
00:54:42,667 --> 00:54:45,489
[SPEAKER_03]: But usually, people just tend to quickly bundle together.

765
00:54:45,529 --> 00:54:46,530
[SPEAKER_03]: Cessation means ending.

766
00:54:46,590 --> 00:54:52,114
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yeah, I can see these branches falling down, trees breaking, everything is impermanent.

767
00:54:52,755 --> 00:54:52,975
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

768
00:54:53,435 --> 00:54:55,137
[SPEAKER_03]: That experience has a reason for you.

769
00:54:56,592 --> 00:55:01,074
[SPEAKER_03]: The experience of a tree being broken down, that's what has arisen in your experience.

770
00:55:01,094 --> 00:55:02,455
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's actually arising, you're looking at.

771
00:55:02,815 --> 00:55:03,015
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

772
00:55:03,256 --> 00:55:06,397
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you don't basically look outside, you just watch your experience.

773
00:55:06,417 --> 00:55:06,657
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

774
00:55:07,178 --> 00:55:07,318
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

775
00:55:07,718 --> 00:55:08,358
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the thing.

776
00:55:08,438 --> 00:55:16,623
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why, like, we're creating a distinction saying, the impermanence that the Buddha was talking about is the impermanence that, if understood, frees you from suffering.

777
00:55:17,183 --> 00:55:25,007
[SPEAKER_03]: And, like, the trees being destroyed makes no difference to you, unless you're emotionally bound with those trees, and then you suffer on contact.

778
00:55:25,765 --> 00:55:28,506
[SPEAKER_03]: But not because they're being destroyed, but because you're attached to them.

779
00:55:28,526 --> 00:55:31,547
[SPEAKER_03]: Because the cause of suffering is not the destruction of things.

780
00:55:31,968 --> 00:55:34,669
[SPEAKER_03]: The cause is your craving in regard to them.

781
00:55:34,889 --> 00:55:40,431
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you would still, even then, look at the wrong place, if you assume the cessation as a destruction.

782
00:55:41,612 --> 00:55:54,677
[SPEAKER_01]: So, our assumption we could think of, for example, correct me if I'm saying wrong, is, okay, there's a situation that I find myself in, living, and within that,

783
00:55:55,715 --> 00:55:58,979
[SPEAKER_01]: There is also a thought of the body in any way.

784
00:55:59,280 --> 00:56:01,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Even just the basic thought of there is the body.

785
00:56:03,365 --> 00:56:10,033
[SPEAKER_01]: And you recognize, you understand, firstly, the nature of matter, of consciousness, basically, that, you know, there.

786
00:56:11,464 --> 00:56:22,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Because all you can have is thoughts, images, dhammas.

787
00:56:22,568 --> 00:56:24,008
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

788
00:56:24,028 --> 00:56:24,769
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what things are.

789
00:56:25,389 --> 00:56:32,071
[SPEAKER_03]: So, well, you can also just, in the... let's say you're mindful of the situation properly, or at least, sort of, sufficiently.

790
00:56:32,891 --> 00:56:39,033
[SPEAKER_03]: If you have a thought like, ah, this body is there, well, see where that thought is directed.

791
00:56:39,133 --> 00:56:39,934
[SPEAKER_03]: What is it aiming at?

792
00:56:40,014 --> 00:56:40,874
[SPEAKER_03]: What is it referring to?

793
00:56:43,221 --> 00:56:45,763
[SPEAKER_03]: What is that you're trying to designate by that thought?

794
00:56:46,424 --> 00:56:48,385
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that's often left unclear to people.

795
00:56:48,645 --> 00:56:51,967
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, you just end up carrying on the assumption they were completely gratuitous to begin with.

796
00:56:52,908 --> 00:56:55,990
[SPEAKER_03]: But because you never looked at their gratuity, you keep keeping them.

797
00:56:56,030 --> 00:56:58,352
[SPEAKER_03]: You keep maintaining them.

798
00:56:58,452 --> 00:57:02,775
[SPEAKER_03]: So, like, when you say, of this body... Well, what am I referring to specifically?

799
00:57:02,975 --> 00:57:04,596
[SPEAKER_03]: Do I refer to the bits that I see with my eyes?

800
00:57:04,736 --> 00:57:08,179
[SPEAKER_03]: Or do I feel this... the feeling of touch of the chair and the table I'm leaning on?

801
00:57:08,880 --> 00:57:12,964
[SPEAKER_03]: Or, or, or the itch, or you feel that they refer to the heat, or the energy, or whatever.

802
00:57:13,224 --> 00:57:14,025
[SPEAKER_03]: What are you referring to?

803
00:57:15,066 --> 00:57:17,908
[SPEAKER_03]: And, just find out what you're referring to, in that general situation.

804
00:57:17,928 --> 00:57:22,772
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, what, what, what is this being directed at, when I say... Because, it has to be directed upon something.

805
00:57:22,852 --> 00:57:27,997
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, if this thing hasn't been arisen at all, you wouldn't be able to pull it in any terms.

806
00:57:28,758 --> 00:57:32,381
[SPEAKER_03]: But, the failure you can pull it, sometimes means you are having some sort of notion of it.

807
00:57:32,841 --> 00:57:34,763
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why it is possible to step back.

808
00:57:43,811 --> 00:57:55,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Theoretically, then, any kind of thing you're referring to, so long as you understand that the actual matter of the body is not that which you are pointing to, could be taken sufficiently to say, this is the body.

809
00:57:56,180 --> 00:57:56,941
[SPEAKER_03]: Sorry, say again?

810
00:57:57,721 --> 00:57:59,162
[SPEAKER_03]: Which matter are you referring to?

811
00:58:00,670 --> 00:58:08,031
[SPEAKER_01]: As long as you understand that whatever you're designating as body is not the matter of the body.

